>> title: Chicken Shawarma
>> category: Smoked & Grilled
>> tags: chicken, middle eastern, oven, shawarma spice, yogurt marinade
>> counters: Shawarma Counter, The Bowl Shop
>> aka: chicken shawarma, shawarma djaj, shish shawarma, shwarma, shawerma, chawarma, chicken doner, chicken döner, chicken gyro, shaved chicken
>> pairs-with: shawarma-spice, white-sauce, pita-bread, toum
>> servings: 6
>> time: 14 hr
>> slack: narrow — twelve hours in the yogurt can be sixteen and the stack at 325°F is patient, but the fifteen minutes at 450°F is blackening the crown and it does not stop there; the two-minute crisp in the pan is the other window, and shavings left in it go from brittle to dry
>> step.1: coat every piece, marinate 12 hr
>> step.2: stack and press into the tin
>> step.3: roast 325°F 1 1/4 hr, then 450°F 15 min
>> step.4: rest 20 min, shave off the block
>> step.5: crisp in the pan 2 min

Coat @boneless chicken thighs{3%lb}(1.4 kg; skin on, opened out flat) piece by piece with @shawarma spice{3%Tbs}(one batch), @whole-milk yogurt{1/2%cup}(120 g), @lemon juice{1/4%cup}(60 mL), @garlic{6%cloves}(crushed), @olive oil{3%Tbs}(45 mL) and @kosher salt{2%tsp}, then cover and ~marinate{12%hr} in the fridge. Thighs and not breast: the stack cooks for well over an hour and breast is dry long before the outside browns.

Stack @&(~1)marinated chicken{} flat in a #loaf tin{}, pressing each piece hard onto the last so there is no air between them, and cap it with @lamb fat{2%oz}(60 g; or two extra skins) and @yellow onion{1%large}(in thick slices). A real spit is never finished — the outside is shaved while the middle is still raw and the stack outlasts the day — so a tin that goes into an oven and comes out again is a different dish wearing the same marinade. This is that dish, and pressing the air out is what lets it be shaved rather than pulled apart.

Roast @&(~1)stack{} in the #oven{} at 325°F (160°C) ~roast{1 1/4%hr}, until a skewer meets no resistance in the middle, then take the heat to 450°F (230°C) for ~roast{15%min} to blacken the crown.

Rest @&(~1)roasted stack{} ~rest{20%min}, turn it out of the tin, and shave it down the sides in thin sheets with a long knife, the way it comes off the cone.

Crisp @&(~1)shavings{} in a very hot #skillet{} in @rendered fat{1%Tbs}(from the tin) ~sear{2%min}, tossing once, until the edges catch and go brittle. That last minute is doing the work the outside of the cone does all day, and skipping it gives you roast chicken.
